        },
        "question": {
          ":question/problem": "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z},$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,1,-2)$ to $(1,-1,-1)?$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "For scalar arc length, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nParametrize the segment and check its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=A+t(B-A) \\\\  &=\\left(t,1 - 2 t,t - 2\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(0)&=\\left(0,1,-2\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(1)&=\\left(1,-1,-1\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le t\\le1$, the velocity and speed are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\left(1,-2,1\\right) \\\\  \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(1)^2+(-2)^2+(1)^2} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute all three coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\sqrt{2(t)+(1 - 2 t)-(t - 2)} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{3 - t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equivalent definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{3 - t})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/id": "898c6184-3f28-5616-abfa-854d55e88554",
