        "question": {
          ":question/problem": "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\sin \\left(\\frac{\u03c0y}{4}\\right)ds$ along the line segment from $(0, 4, -3)$ to $(-3, 2, -5)$.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "For scalar arc length, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nParametrize the segment and check its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=A+t(B-A) \\\\  &=\\left(- 3 t,4 - 2 t,- 2 t - 3\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(0)&=\\left(0,4,-3\\right) \\\\  \\mathbf r(1)&=\\left(-3,2,-5\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le t\\le1$, the velocity and speed are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\left(-3,-2,-2\\right) \\\\  \\|\\mathbf r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(-3)^2+(-2)^2+(-2)^2} \\\\  &=\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute all three coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}(4 - 2 t)\\right) \\\\  &=\\sin{\\left(- \\frac{\\pi t}{2} + \\pi \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equivalent definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\sqrt{17}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sin{\\left(- \\frac{\\pi t}{2} + \\pi \\right)})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $u=- \\frac{\\pi t}{2} + \\pi$ and $\\mathrm{d}u=\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. The new bounds are $u(0)=\\pi$ and $u(1)=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I&=- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{\\pi}\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\sin u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\  &=- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{\\pi}\\left[-\\cos u\\right]_{\\pi}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\  &=- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{\\pi}\\left(-\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+\\cos(\\pi)\\right) \\\\  &=- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{\\pi}\\left((0)-(1)\\right) \\\\  &=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{\\pi}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/id": "61898d77-358f-5558-badf-78f6203e271c",
